'''Saldos''' is a Balboan {{w|off-price retail}} chain operated by [[Grup Wuling]]. Founded in 2001, Saldos sells brand-name clothing, footwear, accessories, and home textiles at discounted prices, typically 30 to 70 per cent below original retail. The chain operates 38 stores across all five provinces of [[Balboa]] and employs approximately 1,400 people. Saldos is the only national off-price retail chain in Balboa and the principal source of branded clothing for consumers in the lower-income interior provinces.

== Concept ==

Saldos operates on the {{w|treasure hunt|treasure-hunt}} off-price retail model, in which inventory changes frequently, sizes and styles are inconsistent, and customers are encouraged to visit regularly to discover new stock. Stores are larger than typical fashion boutiques — averaging 1,200 to 1,800 square metres — and are organised by category rather than by brand, with merchandise displayed on racks and tables without elaborate visual merchandising. The no-frills store format keeps operating costs low and allows the chain to pass savings on to consumers.

Unlike Grup Wuling's full-price franchise stores, which are concentrated in [[Estret Province|Estret]] and [[Jala Province|Jala]], Saldos operates in all five provinces, including locations in [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]] and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] where no full-price brand retail is viable. The chain's presence in the interior provinces makes it the only access point for branded clothing for many consumers, as incomes in these provinces are too low to support new purchases at international price points.

== Inventory sourcing ==

Saldos sources its inventory through four channels:

* '''Franchise excess''' — end-of-season and slow-moving stock from Grup Wuling's own franchise operations, transferred to Saldos rather than marked down in the original stores
* '''Brand overruns and cancellations''' — excess production, cancelled orders, and end-of-line stock purchased directly from international brands at a fraction of wholesale cost
* '''Domestic surplus''' — surplus and second-quality production from domestic textile manufacturers, including [[Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa]] and [[Horaci Eduard Molí]], providing a secondary revenue stream for these manufacturers
* '''Retailer clearance''' — clearance inventory purchased from other Balboan retailers and from BTPMEU-based retailers seeking to liquidate stock

The purchasing team negotiates with international brands and domestic manufacturers for excess stock, often purchasing at 10 to 25 per cent of the original wholesale price. The inventory mix is inconsistent by design — a Saldos store may carry a premium Volisanian brand one week and a mid-range Okaiken label the next, with no guarantee of restocking the same items.

