Store sizes are smaller in the lower-income provinces, reflecting lower merchandise volumes and reduced rent costs. The Tramuntana and Migjorn stores carry a higher proportion of domestic surplus and Grup Wuling's in-house [[Cotidià]] label, as international brand excess is less reliably available in the quantities needed for these markets.

== Pricing ==

Saldos typically offers discounts of 30 to 70 per cent below the original retail price of branded merchandise. The pricing structure is:

* '''Tier 1 (30–40% off)''' — current or recent-season merchandise transferred from Grup Wuling franchise stores, representing the highest-quality and most recognisable stock
* '''Tier 2 (50–60% off)''' — brand overruns, cancelled orders, and end-of-season stock purchased from international brands
* '''Tier 3 (60–70% off)''' — domestic surplus, second-quality goods, and older clearance inventory

The in-house [[Cotidià]] label is sold at fixed prices across all Saldos stores, providing a consistent value option for consumers who may not find branded merchandise in their size or preferred style on a given visit.

== Customer base ==

Saldos's customer base spans a wide income range, but the chain's primary market is the middle and lower-middle segment of the Balboan population — consumers who aspire to brand-name clothing but cannot afford full retail prices. In Estret and Jala, Saldos attracts bargain-hunters and value-conscious shoppers who could afford full-price retail but prefer the off-price model. In Migjorn, Nurra, and Tramuntana, Saldos serves a more fundamental role as the primary source of new clothing for households where disposable income is too limited for full-price purchases.

The chain's presence in the interior provinces has been credited with broadening access to branded clothing for lower-income consumers, though critics note that the inconsistent inventory and limited size ranges can make it difficult for customers to find suitable clothing, and that the treasure-hunt model can encourage unnecessary purchases.

== History ==

Saldos was launched in 2001 by [[Grup Wuling]] as a means of liquidating excess inventory from its growing franchise operations. The first store opened in a suburban retail park on the outskirts of [[Sant Cristòfor]], in a converted warehouse that had previously served as a Grup Wuling distribution depot. The concept proved immediately popular, and the company opened three additional stores in Estret Province within the first year.

The expansion into the interior provinces began in 2003, with stores in [[Sant Bart]] and [[Costabella]]. The interior expansion revealed the depth of demand for affordable branded clothing in the lower-income provinces, and Saldos accelerated its rollout in Migjorn, Nurra, and Tramuntana between 2004 and 2008. By 2008, the chain operated 25 stores nationwide.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis|real-estate crisis]] of the late 2000s and the subsequent economic contraction initially boosted Saldos, as consumers traded down from full-price retail. However, the crisis also reduced the availability of brand excess inventory, as international brands cut production in response to weak demand. Saldos responded by increasing its domestic surplus purchasing and expanding the [[Cotidià]] in-house label to ensure consistent stock levels.

The chain resumed expansion in the 2010s, reaching 38 stores by 2026. Recent growth has focused on the interior provinces, where Saldos continues to be the only national retailer operating in many communities.

